1
00:00:03,183 --> 00:00:05,873
as you get more users, your database grows

2
00:00:05,873 --> 00:00:07,637
and your tables get bigger

3
00:00:07,637 --> 00:00:09,317
now it might take a little bit longer for

4
00:00:09,317 --> 00:00:10,937
your app to load and you're now having

5
00:00:10,937 --> 00:00:11,917
performance issues

6
00:00:11,917 --> 00:00:14,030
it starts at the first row and it checks

7
00:00:14,030 --> 00:00:16,860
every single row, all one million of them,

8
00:00:16,950 --> 00:00:19,320
and that is called a full table scan

9
00:00:19,320 --> 00:00:21,600
when your table has maybe 10, 100 rows,

10
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:23,800
maybe it wouldn't be a problem, maybe you

11
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:24,690
wouldn't notice,

12
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:27,513
but at a million rows, then it's a problem

13
00:00:27,513 --> 00:00:29,403
the easiest way to think about it is the

14
00:00:29,403 --> 00:00:31,257
index at the back of a book

15
00:00:31,257 --> 00:00:32,597
so with this strategy,

16
00:00:32,877 --> 00:00:35,367
even with a million rows, you can get to the

17
00:00:35,367 --> 00:00:38,197
row you're looking for in about 20 steps or

18
00:00:38,197 --> 00:00:40,970
so without an index, you will do up to a

19
00:00:40,970 --> 00:00:43,063
million checks in the worst case scenario

20
00:00:43,063 --> 00:00:45,398
adding a database index will literally up

21
00:00:45,398 --> 00:00:47,543
your query optimisation game, and it's kind

22
00:00:47,543 --> 00:00:48,500
of a one-liner

23
00:00:48,500 --> 00:00:50,540
just put the word EXPLAIN in front of your

24
00:00:50,540 --> 00:00:50,963
query

25
00:00:50,963 --> 00:00:53,483
so now that begs the question, if database

26
00:00:53,483 --> 00:00:56,293
indexes makes queries so fast, why don't we

27
00:00:56,293 --> 00:00:59,230
just index every column and be done with it?

28
00:00:59,230 --> 00:01:01,847
we cannot, because every index has a cost

29
00:01:01,847 --> 00:01:04,257
so the more database indexes you have, the

30
00:01:04,257 --> 00:01:05,673
slower your writes get

31
00:01:05,673 --> 00:01:08,243
another database index gotcha is

32
00:01:08,353 --> 00:01:09,403
that in MySQL,

33
00:01:09,703 --> 00:01:11,613
when you add a foreign key, the database

34
00:01:11,613 --> 00:01:14,433
creates an index on that column for you

35
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:16,453
automatically, if there isn't one already

36
00:01:16,453 --> 00:01:18,493
but in PostgreSQL, it doesn't

37
00:01:18,493 --> 00:01:20,975
and it's one of the common reasons why joins

38
00:01:20,975 --> 00:01:22,583
suddenly get slow

39
00:01:22,583 --> 00:01:24,883
you can have a database index and the

40
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:26,700
database still won't use it
